--TEST--
Cooldown policy falls back to the package-supplied time when the repository provides no published-time, and says so.
--COMPOSER--
{
    "name": "acme/project",
    "version": "1.0.0",
    "require": {
        "acme/library": "1.1.0"
    },
    "config": {
        "policy": {
            "cooldown": {
                "period": "1 week"
            }
        }
    },
    "repositories": [
        {
            "type": "package",
            "package": [
                {
                    "name": "acme/library",
                    "version": "1.1.0",
                    "time": "2999-01-01T00:00:00+00:00",
                    "source": { "reference": "1.1.0", "type": "git", "url": "" }
                }
            ]
        }
    ]
}
--RUN--
update -v

--EXPECT-EXIT-CODE--
2

--EXPECT-OUTPUT--
Loading composer repositories with package information
<warning>1 package version(s) withheld by the cooldown policy (run with -vv to list them).</warning>
Updating dependencies
Your requirements could not be resolved to an installable set of packages.

  Problem 1
    - Root composer.json requires acme/library 1.1.0 (exact version match: 1.1.0 or 1.1.0.0), found acme/library[1.1.0] but these were not loaded, because they have not yet cleared the cooldown configured in "policy.cooldown" (available in %s). The cooldown used the package-supplied release date because the repository did not provide an authoritative publication timestamp. To bypass the cooldown for this package, add it to the "policy.cooldown.ignore" config. To turn the feature off entirely, you can set "policy.cooldown.block" to false.

--EXPECT--
